# Background Remover deliberately does NOT send Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy. Those two headers are what a document needs before
# the browser grants SharedArrayBuffer, and without SharedArrayBuffer ONNX
# Runtime's WASM build stays single-threaded however many cores the visitor has.
# Isolating the route is therefore the obvious way to make the model faster, and
# it has now been tried twice and taken the tool down twice.
#
# The history, because the temptation to try a third time is real:
#
#   1. The headers first shipped duplicated, which browsers reject, so isolation
#      never took effect and nobody noticed — the tool kept working, slowly.
#   2. The duplicate was removed. Isolation took effect for the first time and the
#      tool broke outright. The headers were pulled.
#   3. The thread count turned out to be part of it: the library asks for one
#      thread per logical core, and past a couple of threads it loses to
#      contention badly (measured on four cores, one image: 17.9s at one thread,
#      8.8s at two, 25.9s at sixteen). `plannedThreadCount` caps the request at
#      four, and a threaded attempt that throws is retried single-threaded.
#      Verified on a sixteen-core machine: inference 5.98s against 24.2s.
#   4. It broke again anyway, on a twenty-core machine, with isolation confirmed
#      active, the model manifest served correctly, and four threads planned. The
#      worker died and the single-threaded retry died with it.
#
# Why the fallback cannot save this: it catches a run that *throws*. When the
# browser kills the worker process outright the error event carries no message and
# there is nothing left running to retry into. Step 3's own commit said as much.
#
# So the score is: worked on the one machine it was tuned on, killed the tool on
# both machines that were not. A tool that takes 24s beats a tool that takes
# forever, and this route stays unisolated until someone can reproduce the crash
# and explain it — not merely fail to reproduce it on new hardware.
#
# If it is ever restored: exactly ONE rule may match this page.
# `/tools/background-remover/*` already covers the trailing-slash URL; adding
# `/tools/background-remover/` as well makes both rules match, each policy is sent
# twice, and the joined value `same-origin, same-origin` is invalid — the browser
# falls back to unsafe-none and the page is not isolated at all. That is mistake 1.
